We consider the
following initial boundary value problem for the wave equation with a potential $q$ and an absorption coefficient $a$,
\begin{equation} \left\{
\begin{array}{llll}
\para{\partial^2_t-\Delta+a(x)\partial_t+q(x)}u=0  & \textrm{in }\; Q,\cr
u(\cdot,0 )=\partial_tu(\cdot,0)=0 & \textrm{in }\; \mathrm{M} ,\cr
u=f & \textrm{on } \; \Sigma.
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
Here $a,q:\mathrm{M}\to\mathbb{R}$ are real valued functions in $L^\infty(\mathrm{M})$ and $f\in H^1(\Sigma)$.

\left\{
\begin{array}{llll}
\para{\partial^2_t-\Delta+a(x)\partial_t+q(x)}u=0  & \textrm{in }\; Q,\cr
u(\cdot,0 )=\partial_tu(\cdot,0)=0 & \textrm{in }\; \mathrm{M} ,\cr
u=f & \textrm{on } \; \Sigma.
\end{array}
\right.

